WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:04.643
A deploy starts fresh JVMs.

2
00:00:04.643 --> 00:00:09.595
Until the container has wired its components, no payment goes through.

3
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:14.327
Here is one start, timed from the first line of main to the first payment done.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:17.351
The reflective container does everything now.

5
00:00:17.351 --> 00:00:24.016
It finds the components, reads their annotations, orders the graph, and has Byte Buddy write a subclass.

6
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.827
Ready after 1,029 milliseconds.

7
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:30.635
It loaded 2,207 classes.

8
00:00:31.867 --> 00:00:37.249
The same app, wired by generated code, loads 991 classes.

9
00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:41.161
So 1,216 classes are pure machinery.

10
00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:43.465
814 come from byte-buddy.jar.

11
00:00:43.465 --> 00:00:50.520
Byte Buddy loads its own type model, matchers and class writer before it can write one subclass.

12
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:53.032
Then the 3 classes it writes.

13
00:00:53.032 --> 00:01:01.024
30 JDK proxy classes: one is the issuer proxy, and annotations read by reflection are proxies too.

14
00:01:01.024 --> 00:01:05.428
And 123 hidden classes for lambdas and method handles.

15
00:01:05.533 --> 00:01:11.748
Each one is found, parsed and verified, then run in the interpreter, because nothing is hot yet.

16
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.683
None of this depends on anything but the classes, and those are fixed when you build.

17
00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:20.613
So run the same steps on the build machine, once.

18
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.647
A cycle in the graph now fails the build instead of the deploy.

19
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:33.724
The generator writes plain Java: a holder class for each singleton, and the interceptors as source, with the annotation values written in.

20
00:01:33.867 --> 00:01:34.458
Predict.

21
00:01:34.458 --> 00:01:36.973
What is left for each start to do?

22
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.903
Only the holders.

23
00:01:39.903 --> 00:01:47.723
The JVM initializes each one the first time get touches it, once, under the class initialization lock.

24
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:54.679
Ready after 266 milliseconds, with 991 classes, and no reflection.

25
00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.402
On a busy machine, the times moved by a factor of two between runs.

26
00:01:59.402 --> 00:02:04.920
The reflective start was always several times slower, and the class counts barely moved.

27
00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.311
Here is one holder, in Java and Kotlin.

28
00:02:08.311 --> 00:02:11.973
A plain new call, with its dependency's holder inside.

29
00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:12.780
The price.

30
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:15.755
The set of components is fixed when you build.

31
00:02:15.755 --> 00:02:20.217
And a holder whose constructor throws stays failed until you restart.

32
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:21.628
The rule.

33
00:02:21.628 --> 00:02:25.814
Work that depends only on the classes belongs at build time.

34
00:02:25.814 --> 00:02:28.465
A start should run only ordinary code.

35
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:29.640
Check yourself.

36
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:34.629
In the generated container, where does @Retry's 3 come from at run time?

37
00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:38.946
From the generated source.

38
00:02:38.946 --> 00:02:43.727
The generator read the annotation at build time and wrote 3 into the code.

39
00:02:43.727 --> 00:02:46.311
Nothing reads an annotation at run time.
